<section class="cs-hero container">
    <figure class="sx-cover"><img src="../assets/images/vextaro.png" alt="Vextaro project artwork" width="1200" height="600"></figure>
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="../index.html">De La Paz</a> / <a href="../index.html#work">Work</a> / Vextaro</div>
    <h1>Vextaro</h1>
    <p class="tagline">A men's luxury formalwear rental app. Renters can't try garments on before they ship, so fit uncertainty blocks booking.</p>
    <div class="impact-grid cs-glance"><div class="impact-stat"><div class="n">Tokens</div><div class="l">centralized semantic variables across mobile &amp; web</div></div><div class="impact-stat"><div class="n">9</div><div class="l">loading, empty, and error states designed</div></div><div class="impact-stat"><div class="n">7 + 3</div><div class="l">IA sections and annotated user flows</div></div></div>
    <div class="cs-badge-row">
      <span>Mobile App (iOS &amp; Android)</span>
      <span>Flyrank AI Internship · 8 Weeks</span>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-action-row">
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.figma.com/proto/fwsFyRAMmhGtRbqeYjq0mZ/Vextaro-Internship-Capstone?node-id=2166-2413&viewport=129%2C25%2C0.06&t=AglpdG5iapGW7Pro-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=2166%3A2413&page-id=2166%3A2410"><span>View Figma Prototype <svg class="sx-btn-icon" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.85" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 5.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 1 8.5 2H12v7H8.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 1 5 5.5z"/><path d="M12 2h3.5a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 0 7H12V2z"/><path d="M12 12.5a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 7 0 3.5 3.5 0 1 1-7 0z"/><path d="M5 19.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 1 8.5 16H12v3.5a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-7 0z"/><path d="M5 12.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 1 8.5 9H12v7H8.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 1 5 12.5z"/></svg></span></a>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="../assets/Vextaro_Impact_Project.pdf"><span>View 7-Slide Capstone Deck (PDF) <svg class="sx-btn-icon" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.85" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="18" height="14" x="3" y="3" rx="2"/><path d="M3 12h18"/><path d="m12 17-3 4h6l-3-4"/></svg></span></a>
    </div>
    <dl class="cs-meta">
      <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>UX/UI Design Intern</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Team</dt><dd>Flyrank AI</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Completed</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>Figma, Claude</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-body container">
    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Fit anxiety, not selection</h2>
      <ul><li>10+ competitive audits and desk research (reviews, return rates)</li><li>Renters wanted a second opinion on fit, not more options</li><li>An outfit is a connected system, so fit became the product opportunity</li></ul>
    </div>

    
    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Eight weeks: from blank canvas to engineering hand-off</h2>
      <p>I structured the 8-week capstone around staged milestones, collaborating with Claude and custom tooling each week:</p>
      <div class="timeline-table-wrap">
        <table class="timeline-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Week</th><th>Focus</th><th>Key Deliverable</th><th>AI Toolkit</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>W1</b></td><td>Workspace &amp; Toolkit</td><td>Figma workspace &amp; capstone scope setup</td><td>Claude</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W2</b></td><td>Research Synthesis</td><td>Marcus persona &amp; emotional journey map</td><td>Claude</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W3</b></td><td>IA &amp; Core Flows</td><td>7-section IA &amp; primary booking user flows</td><td>Claude</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W4</b></td><td>"Kill Your Darlings" Cut-down</td><td>Pruned secondary features to protect core fit checkpoint</td><td>Claude</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W5</b></td><td>Wireframes (AI vs Hand-built)</td><td>Hand-built wireframes &amp; fit-check flow</td><td>Claude, Figma AI Agent</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W6</b></td><td>Design System Foundations</td><td>Token variables, 9 components, 35 variants</td><td>Claude, Scripter Scripts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W7</b></td><td>Accessibility &amp; Polish</td><td>Hi-fi screens, 15-check WCAG 2.2 AA audit</td><td>Claude, Scripter Scripts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>W8</b></td><td>Engineering Hand-off Package</td><td>Annotated redlines, component index &amp; tokens</td><td>Claude, Scripter Scripts</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Marcus and his journey</h2>
      <p>Built from desk research: competitor reviews, return-rate data, style guides.</p>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-2">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/Marcus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/Marcus.png" alt="Persona card for Marcus, the primary Vextaro persona" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Marcus, primary persona: high style expectations, low sizing tolerance.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/Emotional%20Journey%20Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/Emotional%20Journey%20Map.png" alt="Emotional journey map for Marcus across the rental experience" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Emotional journey map: pinpointing anxiety spikes at fit submission and unboxing.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-split">
        <div class="cs-split-text">
          <h2>From complaint to checkpoint</h2>
                <div class="reasoning-chain compact">
                  <div class="chain-step">
                    <span class="k">I learned</span>
                    <span class="v">No competitor offered a human-backed fit check before checkout; reviews named the gap.</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="chain-step">
                    <span class="k">So I changed</span>
                    <span class="v">A specialist fit-confirmation checkpoint in booking, plus a 7-section IA and 3 annotated flows (primary, onboarding, recovery with escalation).</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="chain-step">
                    <span class="k">Evaluated via</span>
                    <span class="v">Cognitive walkthroughs confirming the checkpoint is reachable across primary booking and recovery flows without dead ends.</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-split-media">
          <div class="cs-figure-row flex" style="--h:320px">
          <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/fit-check%20wireframe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/fit-check%20wireframe.png" alt="Wireframe of onboarding step 2 of 4, add your measurements, with a link to find a nearby measuring specialist" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Onboarding step 2 of 4: add measurements, or find a nearby measuring specialist.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Information architecture &amp; recovery flows</h2>
      <p>I structured a 7-section information architecture to organize formalwear cataloging, fitting appointments, and booking milestones, paired with a dedicated recovery user flow with specialist escalation paths.</p>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-1">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/IA.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/IA.png" alt="Vextaro seven-section information architecture" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Information architecture: 7 core product sections mapping the renter journey.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-2">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/recovery%20flow.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/recovery%20flow.png" alt="Vextaro recovery user flow with escalation" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Recovery user flow: handling sizing mismatches with specialist escalation.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/onboarding.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/onboarding.png" alt="Vextaro onboarding welcome screen" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Onboarding welcome flow: introducing the fit specialist guarantee upfront.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>High-fidelity screens</h2>
      <p>From welcome to checkout, built on the token system: onboarding with a fit quiz, sign-in, occasion-first discovery, filtered cataloging, and rent-or-buy product pages with a sticky action bar.</p>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-2">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-welcome.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-welcome.png" alt="Vextaro welcome screen" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Welcome screen: establishing luxury positioning and fit-first guarantee.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-sign-in.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-sign-in.png" alt="Vextaro sign-in screen" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Sign-in: with inline validation states and clear secondary pathways.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-2">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-home.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-home.png" alt="Vextaro home screen starting from the occasion" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Home screen: browsing organized by event type (Black Tie, Wedding, Gala).</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-product.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-product.png" alt="Vextaro product page with rent or buy choice" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Product page: rent-vs-buy toggle with dynamic size &amp; pricing in the sticky bar.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-split">
        <div class="cs-split-text">
          <h2>Measurements, sized the way clothes are</h2>
          <p>A measurements editor replaces typing numbers. Each field steps the way that item is actually sized.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Eight measurements: height, chest, neck, sleeve, waist, inseam, shoe size, hat size</li>
            <li>Steps of 1 inch, 0.5 for neck and shoe size, 1/8 for hat size</li>
            <li>Minus and plus buttons with a clear Cancel and Confirm</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-split-media"><div class="cs-figure-row cols-1"><figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-measurements.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-measurements.png" alt="Vextaro measurements editor" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Measurements editor.</figcaption></figure></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-split">
        <div class="cs-split-text">
          <h2>An AI fit estimate, as a concept</h2>
          <p>Vextaro has no automated AI sizing today. I designed one as a labeled concept to explore how algorithmic suggestions can build user trust through transparency rather than unearned authority.</p>
          <div class="reasoning-chain compact">
            <div class="chain-step">
              <span class="k">I learned</span>
              <span class="v">Renters distrust automated sizing when presented as definitive truth; wrong sizes for milestone events carry high emotional stakes.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chain-step">
              <span class="k">So I changed</span>
              <span class="v">Labeled the recommendation as an "AI estimate" with confidence scores, transparent rationale ("Why this size?"), and an escape hatch to specialist review.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chain-step">
              <span class="k">Evaluated via</span>
              <span class="v">Flow walkthroughs ensuring renters can edit measurements or request human specialist confirmation at any stage without dead ends.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <ul>
            <li>Explicit confidence ratings with understandable reasoning.</li>
            <li>Always correctable: "Edit measurements", "Not my size", or "Ask a specialist".</li>
            <li>Error states for low confidence and slow server responses.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-split-media"><div class="cs-figure-row cols-1"><figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-fit-estimate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-fit-estimate.png" alt="Vextaro AI fit estimate concept screen" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Fit estimate concept: medium confidence, with reasons and an escape hatch.</figcaption></figure></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Designed for the unhappy path</h2>
      <p>Catalog, cart, and wardrobe each have dedicated loading, empty, and error states so no user journey ends at a dead-end blank screen:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Catalog states:</strong> Skeleton shimmer loading, zero-results filter reset, and API network failure with offline retry.</li>
        <li><strong>Cart &amp; Wardrobe states:</strong> Empty garment shelf with direct CTA to occasion-based browsing, reserved-item expiration timer, and payment retry.</li>
        <li><strong>Prototype coverage:</strong> All 9 states wired into a clickable high-fidelity Figma flow: welcome, quiz, occasion home, filtered catalog, product details, fit-check, and cart.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Accessibility pass, fixed at the token level</h2>
      <p>I computed contrast from token hex values, then audited 15 checks against WCAG 2.2 AA standards: 7 passed initially, 5 were resolved at the token level, and 3 engineering edge cases were documented.</p>
      <div class="reasoning-chain compact">
        <div class="chain-step">
          <span class="k">I discovered</span>
          <span class="v">Disabled gray (#9CA3AF) on white produced a 4.29:1 contrast ratio, failing WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1 minimum) when reused for secondary labels.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="chain-step">
          <span class="k">So I changed</span>
          <span class="v">Created a dedicated secondary-text token (#52525B at 8.31:1 ratio) and expanded interactive row heights from 16px to 40px touch targets.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="chain-step">
          <span class="k">Evaluated via</span>
          <span class="v">Automated hex-to-contrast calculations across all token variables and component states.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-1">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-accessibility-audit.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/ui-accessibility-audit.png" alt="Vextaro accessibility audit table: 15 checks with result, evidence and fix" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Accessibility audit table: 15 WCAG 2.2 AA checks documented with specific token-level resolutions.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Engineering hand-off</h2>
      <p>The final design deliverable is an engineering hand-off specification package built so developers can implement screens without ambiguity. Five key screens are annotated with numbered redlines, responsive behavior, and edge cases:</p>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-2">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-annotated-product.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-annotated-product.png" alt="Annotated Vextaro product page with numbered markers and a table of size, spacing, behavior and edge cases" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Product page spec: numbered markers, spacing, behavior, and edge cases.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-annotated-fit-check.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-annotated-fit-check.png" alt="Annotated Vextaro AI fit check screen with a table of size, spacing and behavior" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Fit check spec: AI estimate is labeled, correctable, and never a single point of failure.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Annotated screens:</strong> Product page, fit check, measurements editor, sign-in (with error state), and cart checkout.</li>
        <li><strong>Component index:</strong> Comprehensive table documenting 9 master components and 35 variants with interaction guidelines.</li>
        <li><strong>Production asset exports:</strong> Vector SVGs and raster PNGs configured at 1x, 2x, and 3x densities.</li>
        <li><strong>Prototype QA:</strong> Complete navigation flow validated with zero dead-ends or broken transitions across all branches.</li>
        <li><strong>Documented edge cases:</strong> Open architectural decisions explicitly logged in Figma for engineering review.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="cs-figure-row cols-1">
        <figure><a class="cs-zoom" href="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-component-index.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="../assets/images/case-studies/vextaro/handoff-component-index.png" alt="Vextaro component index table: component, variants, states, usage" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Component index: variant architecture and state usage guidelines.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    
    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>AI integration log: Three scripts that saved hours, one that taught me more</h2>
      <p>Rather than letting AI generate arbitrary designs, I wrote Scripter scripts (JavaScript via the Figma Plugin API) and directed Claude to automate repetitive, error-prone production tasks:</p>
      
      <div class="ai-tools-grid">
        <div class="ai-card">
          <span class="ai-badge">Time-Saver 1</span>
          <h3>Assemble screens from components</h3>
          <p>Wrote a Scripter script to programmatically assemble 4 new views and 9 state screens directly from master components, followed by manual design QA on every screen.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ai-card">
          <span class="ai-badge">Time-Saver 2</span>
          <h3>Semantic layer renaming for hand-off</h3>
          <p>Automated consistent layer naming across the entire file so developers receive clean, semantic frame trees without hours of manual renaming.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ai-card">
          <span class="ai-badge">Time-Saver 3</span>
          <h3>Automated token contrast audit</h3>
          <p>Extracted token hex values into Claude to calculate WCAG contrast ratios. Exposed a failing light-mode secondary text value (4.29:1) and resolved it at the token level (8.31:1).</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ai-lesson-box">
        <h4><span class="alert-icon">⚠</span> The Failed Prompt &amp; Technical Lesson</h4>
        <p>An AI-generated script called <code>figma.createAutoLayout</code>, which does not exist in the Figma Plugin API, causing a runtime crash. I diagnosed the error, debugged the script, and replaced it with a custom <code>createFrame</code> helper. <strong>Lesson:</strong> Always test automation scripts on isolated sandbox frames before running them globally.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>A system built to scale</h2>
      <p>Rather than designing disconnected mockups, I established a centralized Figma token architecture to ensure consistency, accessibility, and smooth developer handoff across the entire rental experience:</p>
      
      <div class="impact-grid system-metrics">
        <div class="impact-stat">
          <div class="n">50%+</div>
          <div class="stat-title">Component Consolidation</div>
          <div class="l">Cut 24 redundant styles into 9 master components with 35 interactive variants</div>
        </div>
        <div class="impact-stat">
          <div class="n">15 / 15</div>
          <div class="stat-title">WCAG 2.2 AA Audited</div>
          <div class="l">Zero contrast failures; secondary text resolved at 8.31:1 ratio across all tokens</div>
        </div>
        <div class="impact-stat">
          <div class="n">3 Flows</div>
          <div class="stat-title">Zero-Dead-End Recovery</div>
          <div class="l">Dedicated escalation paths for fit mismatches, date conflicts, and measurement overrides</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="system-takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Token hierarchy:</strong> Centralized Figma variables mapping primitive hex values to semantic roles (surface, brand, text, interactive states) across light and dark contexts.</li>
          <li><strong>Interactive variants:</strong> Standardized states (default, hover, active, focus, error, disabled) across bottom sheets, occasion selector chips, and sticky booking bars.</li>
          <li><strong>Production-ready handoff:</strong> Complete component index paired with numbered spatial redlines so frontend engineers can build without guesswork.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


    
    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Three learnings and one hard problem</h2>
      <div class="reflection-grid">
        <div class="reflection-card">
          <h3>1. Fix it at the token level</h3>
          <p>A single contrast fix in a token corrected every component and variant across light and dark modes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reflection-card">
          <h3>2. AI builds fast, I decide</h3>
          <p>Exercised strict editorial restraint: rejected an arbitrary AI type-scale rescale, rejected a full-width Swap button, and rejected synthetic AI imagery in favor of real curation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reflection-card">
          <h3>3. Design the unhappy path</h3>
          <p>Uncertain, slow-loading, and error correction states matter as much as the happy path.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="challenge-box">
        <h3>The Hard Problem: Trust as a design hypothesis</h3>
        <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> Making an AI fit estimate trustworthy in a conceptual prototype without an active production machine learning model.</p>
        <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Clearly labeled the recommendation as an "AI estimate", exposed transparent reasoning ("Why this size?"), and provided immediate manual escape hatches (Edit measurements, Not my size, Ask a specialist). Because it is not yet tested in live production, trust remains a rigorous design hypothesis to be validated with real renters.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-section">
      <h2>Production readiness & next steps</h2>
      <p>With the core token architecture, accessibility audit, and engineering handoff completed, the project is structured for production implementation. Next steps for the engineering rollout:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Engineering alignment:</strong> Review open edge-case decisions in Figma (such as dynamic sticky-bar size display vs. unconfirmed fit lock at checkout).</li>
        <li><strong>Token compilation:</strong> Export Figma variables into design token JSON / CSS custom properties for direct ingestion into the frontend codebase.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-launch usability testing:</strong> Track checkout drop-off rates on the specialist fit checkpoint to validate reduction in fit-related rental returns.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

